Configure the Trunk Port
------------------------
           VLAN 10
PC0 ----------\
               \
                Switch -------- Router
               /
PC1 ----------/
           VLAN 20

Question:
---------
    - PC0 sends data
    - the switch receives it
    - now the switch has to send packet to the router

    Question: How will the router know:
    1) is this packet from VLAN 10
    2) is this packet from VLAN 20

    - A normal Ethernet frame does not contain the VLAN ID
    - So if we simply send it over a normal link, the router cannot identify which VLAN it belongs to

Solution:
---------
    - cisco uses a standard called IEEE 802.1Q
    - when a frame leaves the switch through a trunk port, the switch adds
      VLAN tag to the Ethernet Frame

What is a Trunk:
----------------
    - An access port carries traffic for only one VLAN
    Example: PC -------- Switch ----- Access Port

    - A trunk port carries traffic for multiple VLANs
    Example: Switch ------- Router ------> carries VLAN 10, VLAN 20, VLAN 30, VLAN 100

    that's why the switch to router link must be a trunk


Configure the Trunk:
--------------------
enable
configure terminal
interface fastEthernet0/24
switchport mode trunk (this interface is no longer an access port)
no shutdown
exit
end
write memory

    verify: show interfaces trunk

What actually happens:
----------------------
    - suppose PC0 sends a frame:

    inside switch:
    --------------
    PC0 -----> (Normal Ethernet Frame)
    Switch --> (Add VLAN tag (VLAN 10))
    ========= Trunk ===========
    Router

    PC1
     |
     |  (Normal Ethernet Frame)
     |
    Switch
     |
     |  Adds VLAN Tag (VLAN 20)
     |
    ========== Trunk ==========
     |
    Router

    * The PCs never see the VLAN tag:
    - the tag exists only on the trunk link between network devices (switches and routers)
    - before the frame is delivered to the destination PC, the switch removes the VLAN tag

Q: Why do we configure a trunk between the switch and the router:
-----------------------------------------------------------------
    - A trunk port is used to carry traffic from multiple VLANs over a single physical link,
    - It used IEEE 802 1Q VLAN tagging, so that the receiving device can identify which VLAN each frame belongs to

Will the router be able to distinguish VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 traffic:
-------------------------------------------------------------------
    - no, because without a trunk, the switch sends normal Ethernet frame
    - A normal Ethernet frame looks like this:
        +-------------------------+
        | Source MAC              |
        | Destination MAC         | # there is no VLAN ID
        | Data                    |
        +-------------------------+

Why did Cisco invent trunking instead of using one cable per VLAN:
------------------------------------------------------------------
    Imagine you have:
    -----------------
    VLAN 10
    VLAN 20
    VLAN 30
    VLAN 40
    VLAN 50

    Without trunking, you would need:
    ---------------------------------
    Switch ---------------- Router
    VLAN10 ---- Cable 1
    VLAN20 ---- Cable 2
    VLAN30 ---- Cable 3
    VLAN40 ---- Cable 4
    VLAN50 ---- Cable 5

    Now imagine a data center with:
    -------------------------------
    100 VLANs
    500 VLANs
    1000 VLANs

    Would you install 1000 physical cables?
    Impossible,
    It would waste: Switch ports, Router ports, Cables, Rack space, Money
















